ADF (Augmented Dickey-Fuller) Test is a statistical test used to determine whether a time series is stationary or non-stationary. 
If the p-value is less than 0.05, we reject the null hypothesis
and conclude that the series is stationary. It is commonly used before applying ARIMA and other forecasting models.

=========

Software computes:

Test Statistic
p-value
Critical Values

Usually using Python:
-------------------
from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller
result = adfuller(data)
print("ADF Statistic:", result[0])
print("p-value:", result[1])
--------------------

Decision Rule
Case 1
Value	Result
p-value = 0.01	Less than 0.05

Reject H₀

✅ Data is Stationary

Case 2
Value	Result
p-value = 0.30	Greater than 0.05

Fail to Reject H₀

❌ Data is Non-Stationary

================

Real-Time Example 1: Stock Prices

| Day | Price |
| --- | ----- |
| 1   | 100   |
| 2   | 105   |
| 3   | 110   |
| 4   | 120   |
| 5   | 130   |


Prices keep increasing.

ADF often gives:

p-value = 0.45

Since:

0.45 > 0.05

Result:

❌ Non-Stationary

============

In Python

-----------
from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller
data = [100,105,110,120,130]
result = adfuller(data)
print("ADF Statistic:", result[0])
print("p-value:", result[1])
